CAT = "ukfawBleed"; SEG = "BLD"
Q = [
("A worker has a deep cut to the forearm that is bleeding heavily. What should you do first?",
 "Apply firm direct pressure", ["Apply a tourniquet first", "Rinse the wound out well", "Wait for it to clot by itself"],
 "Firm direct pressure on the wound, ideally with a sterile or haemostatic dressing, is the first step for severe bleeding. A tourniquet is for limb bleeding that direct pressure cannot control."),
("Blood is seeping through the first dressing on a wound. What should you do?",
 "Add a second dressing", ["Take it off and rinse", "Loosen it slightly", "Put ice on top of it"],
 "Leave the first dressing in place and apply a second over it, keeping pressure on. Removing it disturbs any clot that has started to form."),
("Where should a tourniquet be applied on a bleeding limb?",
 "5 to 7 cm above the wound", ["Directly over the wound", "Just below the wound site", "Over the nearest joint"],
 "Resuscitation Council UK says 5 to 7 cm above the injury, between the wound and the heart, and never over a joint."),
("You have applied a tourniquet. What should you do next?",
 "Note the time it went on", ["Loosen it every 10 minutes", "Remove it after 20 minutes", "Cover it with clothing"],
 "Write down the time of application and tell the ambulance crew. Do not loosen or remove it; a second tourniquet may be added above if bleeding continues."),
("A piece of metal is embedded in a casualty's thigh. What should you do?",
 "Pad round it, leave it in", ["Pull it out slowly and gently", "Press down hard on it", "Cut it off at the skin"],
 "The object may be plugging the wound, so leave it in place. Press on either side of it and build padding around it before bandaging."),
("Which signs suggest a casualty is going into shock?",
 "Pale, cold, clammy skin", ["Flushed, hot and dry skin", "Slow, strong pulse", "Deep, slow breathing"],
 "In shock the body diverts blood from the skin to vital organs, so the skin turns pale, cold and clammy. The pulse becomes fast and weak, and breathing fast and shallow."),
("How does the pulse usually change as shock develops?",
 "It becomes fast and weak", ["It becomes slow and strong", "It becomes slow and weak", "It stays normal and steady"],
 "The heart speeds up to make up for the lost volume, but each beat is weaker. A slowing pulse late in shock is a very serious sign."),
("How should you position a casualty in shock from blood loss?",
 "Lay them down, keep warm", ["Sit them up with a drink", "Walk them to fresh air", "Lay them face down flat"],
 "Lay the casualty down, treat the cause and keep them warm with a blanket. Walking or sitting them up makes it harder for blood to reach the brain."),
("A casualty in shock asks for a drink. What should you give them?",
 "Nothing, just wet the lips", ["A mug of hot, sweet tea", "A large glass of cold water", "A small sip of brandy"],
 "Give nothing by mouth because they may vomit or need an anaesthetic. You can moisten their lips if they are thirsty."),
("Hypovolaemic shock is caused by:",
 "A loss of body fluid", ["A severe allergic reaction", "The heart failing to pump", "An injury to the spine"],
 "Hypovolaemic means low blood volume, from bleeding, burns or severe vomiting and diarrhoea. Anaphylaxis and heart failure cause other kinds of shock."),
("How should you treat a nosebleed?",
 "Lean forward, pinch the soft part", ["Tilt head back, pinch the bridge", "Lie flat, pinch the bridge", "Tilt back, press the cheeks"],
 "Leaning forward stops blood running down the throat, and pinching the soft part just below the bony bridge closes the bleeding vessels."),
("For how long should a casualty pinch their nose to stop a nosebleed?",
 "About 10 minutes", ["About 1 minute", "About 30 seconds", "About 3 minutes"],
 "Pinch continuously for about 10 minutes without letting go to check. Releasing early disturbs the clot."),
("How should you look after a severed finger until the ambulance arrives?",
 "Wrap, bag and keep it cool", ["Put it straight onto ice", "Soak it in a cup of water", "Wrap it in a warm towel"],
 "Wrap the part in cling film or a clean dressing, seal it in a bag and surround the bag with ice or cold packs. Ice directly on tissue damages it."),
("How should you treat a small, clean cut?",
 "Rinse, dry and cover it", ["Cover it without rinsing", "Pack it with cotton wool", "Rub antiseptic cream in"],
 "Rinse under clean running water, pat dry and cover with a plaster or sterile dressing. Cotton wool sheds fibres into the wound."),
("A graze has small bits of grit in it. What should you do?",
 "Rinse under running water", ["Dig grit out with a pin", "Scrub it with a stiff brush", "Cover it and leave it"],
 "Gently rinsing under clean running water flushes out loose dirt. Anything firmly embedded should be left for a medical professional."),
("How should you remove a small splinter that sticks out of the skin?",
 "Clean tweezers, same angle", ["Squeeze until it pops out", "Cut it out with a blade", "Push it fully under the skin"],
 "Grip the splinter close to the skin with clean tweezers and draw it out at the angle it went in. Squeezing can break it and leave pieces behind."),
("How can you reduce swelling from a bruise?",
 "A cold pack in a cloth", ["A hot water bottle", "Ice straight on the skin", "Rubbing it firmly"],
 "A cold compress or ice pack wrapped in a cloth reduces bleeding under the skin. Ice on bare skin can cause a cold burn."),
("Which sign suggests internal bleeding after a fall?",
 "Shock but no visible wound", ["A small graze on one knee", "A red, itchy rash on the arm", "Normal colour and pulse"],
 "Shock with no obvious external blood loss should make you suspect bleeding inside the body. Call 999 and treat for shock."),
("Part of the bowel is showing through an abdominal wound. What should you do?",
 "Cover it, do not push it back", ["Push it gently back inside", "Wash it under the tap first", "Leave it open to the fresh air"],
 "Cover the wound with a large sterile or damp clean dressing and do not touch or push the organs back. Call 999 and treat for shock."),
("What does a haemostatic dressing do?",
 "Helps blood to clot", ["Cools the wound down", "Numbs the pain", "Kills all bacteria"],
 "A haemostatic dressing contains an agent that speeds clotting. The 2025 UK guidelines suggest it for severe bleeding with firm direct pressure."),
("Why do scalp wounds often bleed heavily?",
 "The scalp has many blood vessels", ["The skull is very thin there", "Hair stops blood clotting", "Head wounds are always very deep"],
 "The scalp has a rich blood supply, so even a small cut can bleed a lot. Apply pressure and check for a head injury."),
("A worker cuts their hand on a rusty, soil-covered tool. Which protection should they check is up to date?",
 "Tetanus", ["Hepatitis A", "Measles", "Influenza"],
 "Soil and dirty objects can carry the bacteria that cause tetanus. Advise them to see a doctor or nurse to check their tetanus cover."),
("Which signs suggest a wound has become infected?",
 "Redness, heat and pus", ["A pale, dry, numb edge", "A thin scab that itches", "A bruise around the cut"],
 "Increasing redness, warmth, swelling, pain or pus are signs of infection, and the casualty should see a doctor. A forming scab is part of normal healing."),
("A casualty is breathing but bleeding heavily from a leg wound. What is your priority?",
 "Control the bleeding", ["Check for broken bones", "Take a full history", "Clean the wound"],
 "Life-threatening bleeding is dealt with straight away, alongside the airway and breathing. A detailed check for other injuries comes later in the secondary survey."),
("How can you help a casualty in shock to breathe more easily?",
 "Loosen tight clothing", ["Give them a hot drink", "Raise their head high", "Ask them to walk"],
 "Loosen clothing at the neck, chest and waist so it does not restrict breathing or blood flow. Never give drinks to a casualty in shock."),
("A bandage on the arm is finished. What should you check?",
 "Circulation beyond it", ["The bandage's colour", "The casualty's temperature", "The pulse in the neck"],
 "Check the fingers below the bandage for colour, warmth and feeling. Pale, cold or numb fingers mean it is too tight and must be loosened."),
("You press on a fingernail below a bandage and release. How quickly should colour return?",
 "Within 2 seconds", ["Within 10 seconds", "Within 30 seconds", "Within 1 minute"],
 "The nail bed should pink up within about two seconds. A slower capillary refill means the bandage is too tight or circulation is poor."),
("Blood is spurting from a wound in time with the pulse. What does this suggest?",
 "A damaged artery", ["A damaged vein", "A damaged capillary", "A minor graze"],
 "Arterial blood is bright red and spurts with each heartbeat. Venous blood flows steadily and is darker."),
("A nosebleed has not stopped after 30 minutes of pinching. What should you do?",
 "Get them to A&E", ["Tilt the head back", "Pinch for another hour", "Pack it with tissue"],
 "A nosebleed that will not stop with firm pinching needs hospital treatment. Tilting the head back sends blood down the throat."),
("What is the main risk of using a tourniquet that is too loose?",
 "Bleeding can get worse", ["It causes a cold burn", "It traps the clot inside", "It numbs the wound"],
 "A loose tourniquet blocks the veins but not the artery, so blood flows in and cannot get out. Tighten it until the bleeding stops."),
("Why should you avoid cotton wool on an open wound?",
 "Fibres stick in the wound", ["It is not sterile enough", "It stops the blood clotting", "It soaks up too little"],
 "Loose fibres stick to the wound and are hard to remove, raising the infection risk. Use a non-fluffy sterile dressing instead."),
("A casualty is bleeding from a varicose vein in the leg. What should you do?",
 "Lay them down, press firmly", ["Sit them up on a chair", "Apply a tourniquet above it", "Put a warm pad on the leg"],
 "Lay the casualty down, raise the leg and apply firm direct pressure. Varicose vein bleeds can be heavy but usually respond to pressure."),
]
